# Community W4A16 G128 quant of Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (aixiaoma/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-W4A16,
# downloaded to /srv/llm/aixiaoma/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-W4A16, 168 GiB on disk).
# Same compressed-tensors W4A16 group-128 scheme as our own RTN quant (68.4 GiB
# GPU-resident / 99 GiB PLE table), but produced by a third party and (unlike
# our own quantizer) does NOT hit the fused-QKV/gate_up parameter-registration
# bug we found in our own RTN quants (tools/quant_qwen38.py, tools/quant_flashnext.py) —
# verified working 2026-08-28.
#
# Currently the ONLY model running (27b is stopped while we bring this up +
# tune it). At util=0.95, standalone: 19.95 GiB/GPU model weights. Pre-patch
# (stock Triton WNA16 MoE) this left ~6.4 GiB overhead, enough for the full
# native 262144 ctx (276,497-token GPU KV pool measured — hybrid GDN
# architecture means KV cost is dominated by the periodic full-attention
# layers, not linear per-token cost, so full context fits in far less
# headroom than a dense model would need).
#
# 2026-08-28: applied patches/vllm-flashnext/43389-moe-int4-interleave-rocm.patch
# (upstream vllm-project/vllm#43389, unmerged — AMD-authored Triton MoE int4
# interleave-unpack kernel, RDNA-gated via on_rdna()). Measured on this stack:
# pp2048 700-780 -> 2344-2346 t/s (~3x), tg32/tg128 unchanged within noise
# (17.88 vs 16.95, 15.52 vs 16.07). Coherence + multi-turn prefix-cache probe
# both PASS. Tradeoff: the patched kernel's profiling pass uses ~3.5-4 GiB
# more peak activation memory (model weight footprint unchanged at 19.95
# GiB/GPU), which drops available KV cache and caps usable ctx at ~158304
# instead of 262144 -- VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN below is cut to 155000 to leave
# headroom. Revert to 262144 + drop the patch if the reduced context matters
# more than the pp2048 win for a given workload; watch upstream PR #43389 for
# merge (may get a lower-overhead revision).
#
# 2026-08-29: KV-pool audit + recovery (env-only, no rebuild). Investigated
# why the KV pool felt small: per-GPU budget (31.86 GiB) breaks down as
# weights 19.95 + non-torch 6.86 (RCCL/hipBLASLt/aiter) + peak-activation
# ~1.5 + cudagraph 0.41, leaving KV ~2.5 GiB. Confirmed the PLE table IS on
# host (~131 GiB pinned) and the W4A16 quant is correct -- NEITHER is the
# limiter. Also re-read #43389: it is memory-NEUTRAL by construction (int32
# repack is the same byte count as the uint8 packing; it never materializes
# fp16 weights and does not resize the MoE activation cache), so the "3.5-4
# GiB" note above is a TRANSIENT autotune spike during the memory-profiling
# pass, now suppressed by the Track A tuned fused_moe_configs (commit
# 60a0ff0). The real KV limiter is just the fixed weight + non-torch
# footprint. Recovered context with env knobs (validated on giant-server,
# back-to-back reboots, coherence PASS each time):
#   baseline  (util 0.97, batched 4096):            172,302 tok / 1.11x conc
#   THIS      (util 0.98, batched 2048):            202,581 tok / 1.31x conc  (+17.6%, full OOM margin)
#   aggressive(+ VLLM_MEMORY_PROFILER_ESTIMATE_CUDAGRAPHS=0): 236,465 tok / 1.53x conc (+37%)
# Adopted the safe middle (util 0.98 + batched 2048): the cudagraph memory
# (0.41 GiB) stays accounted for, so no OOM-margin risk. For max context add
# VLLM_MEMORY_PROFILER_ESTIMATE_CUDAGRAPHS=0 (opt-in): it stops reserving the
# 0.41 GiB cudagraph headroom -> ~236k tok, but boots with a thin margin
# (vLLM warns to lower util to avoid OOM under load). batched 4096->2048 only
# moved peak activation ~0.1 GiB here (MoE activation is not the dominant
# peak) but slightly tightens long-prompt prefill chunking; kept at 2048
# since pp2048 is unaffected and it's a small net KV win.
#
# 2026-08-29: NEGATIVE RESULT -- do NOT set PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=
# expandable_segments:True on this stack. It looked like a huge win at boot
# (consumed 26.78 -> 20.51 GiB/GPU, non-torch ~7.07 -> ~0.81 GiB/GPU, KV pool
# 202,581 -> 632,255 tok / 4.08x conc) and captured CUDA graphs without error,
# BUT the served output is DEGENERATE GARBAGE: greedy decode of "17 times 3"
# returned 1200 tokens of "...Engine Engine Engine..." (finish=length, empty
# content), reproduced on the raw /v1/completions endpoint too. Root-caused
# 2026-08-29: it is an ALLOCATOR-LEVEL bug in the HIP expandable-segments
# implementation on ROCm 7.14/gfx1201/torch 2.13 -- NOT a CUDA-graph
# interaction. It still produces garbage with CUDA graphs fully DISABLED
# (cudagraph_mode:NONE, "0.0 GiB for CUDAGraph memory" in the boot log, pure
# eager), and the pytorch#158029 NCCL-registration workaround NCCL_GRAPH_REGISTER=0
# does NOT help either. Track the upstream MemPool+expandable-segments interop
# fix (pytorch#147851) and re-test on future ROCm/torch bumps. Reverting to the
# default allocator restores coherence (finish=stop, correct "51").
# NCCL_BUFFSIZE=2097152 was co-tested and is harmless but gave no measurable
# win on its own; not adopted.
#
# 2026-08-29: POSITIVE RESULT -- reclaimed the SAME ~6.3 GiB/GPU headroom
# SAFELY via PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=max_split_size_mb:512 (set below). This
# caps the HIP caching allocator's block-split size so it stops holding the
# large over-reserved segments the profiling forward pass leaves cached
# (counted as non-torch), WITHOUT the segment-remapping that makes
# expandable_segments corrupt memory. Result: consumed 26.78 -> 20.45 GiB/GPU,
# KV pool 202,581 -> 635,139 tok, concurrency 1.31x -> 4.10x, CUDA graphs ON
# (0.41 GiB captured), output COHERENT (single + 4 concurrent). Perf within
# run-to-run noise: pp2048 ~2400 t/s unchanged; tg32/tg128 vary 66-83 t/s in
# BOTH this config and the no-knob control (environmental), with a same-session
# 82.83 t/s tg32 sample confirming no systematic decode regression. Adding
# garbage_collection_threshold:0.9 on top gave no extra memory and only added
# decode jitter, so it was dropped -- max_split_size_mb:512 alone is the win.
#
# 2026-08-28: applied patches/vllm-flashnext/53899-short-conv-async-h2d-rocm.patch
# (sourced from a #53899 review comment; that PR itself is unrelated CUDA-only
# PLE offload). Routes 5 CPU->GPU req-idx copies in the PLE ShortConv
# attention metadata builder through async_tensor_h2d (pinned staging)
# instead of a plain .to(device) off a fresh .nonzero() (pageable memory).
# Reported upstream as 33.2ms -> 5.1ms per worker step on 2x MI210 (gfx90a).
# Measured on this stack (llama-benchy pp2048/tg32+128, 3 runs): tg32 17.12
# ± 2.78 vs baseline 16.95-17.88, tg128 14.67 ± 1.28 vs baseline 15.52-16.07
# -- within run-to-run noise, no measurable win on gfx1201/RDNA4. Kept
# anyway: it's a strict correctness/perf improvement (removes a synchronous
# pageable-memory copy) with zero measured regression and no other tradeoff;
# the eager-mode per-token compute cost dominates here, not this metadata
# builder's host copies. Applies regardless of PLE_CPU_OFFLOAD vs PLE_MMAP.
#
# torch.compile (mode 3 / VLLM_COMPILE) still crashes with a dynamo
# shape-specialization error on query_start_loc for this hybrid GDN
# architecture (a torch._check() patch for one such crash was prototyped
# 2026-08-28 but then hit a separate, harder blocker: Inductor's
# compile/autotune scratch memory OOMs on this 68 GiB/TP4 model regardless
# of context length, cudagraph settings, or gpu_memory_utilization — proven
# by three isolating tests, so full compile is not pursued further).
#
# 2026-08-28: CUDA graphs do NOT require torch.compile, though: mode stays
# 0 (no compile) but cudagraph_mode=FULL_DECODE_ONLY captures prefill+decode
# graphs directly in eager mode, eliminating most of the per-op kernel-
# launch overhead that was capping decode throughput. Measured (fresh A/B,
# same conditions): tg32 18.26±1.90 -> 74.07±5.13 tok/s (~4.0x), tg128
# 15.07±1.79 -> 69.68±10.59 tok/s (~4.4x). pp2048 unaffected (2340 vs 2380
# tok/s, within noise). Coherence/stability verified: 5x short + 1x
# ~1200-token generation, no crashes, no OOM. Cudagraph capture buffers
# need a small amount of extra headroom beyond the eager-mode budget — at
# util=0.95 the KV cache falls just short at full 155000 ctx (a graceful
# ValueError, not a crash: 2.31 GiB needed vs 1.93 GiB available, estimated
# max ctx 125664); util=0.97 (below) restores full 155000 ctx. See
# benchmarks/2026-08-28_qwen3.8-flash-next_cudagraph_decode_only_bench.md.
# PLE table lives in pinned host RAM (~99 GiB, VLLM_PLE_CPU_OFFLOAD=1 — host
# has 228 GiB available).
#
# When re-adding the 27b service alongside this: needs ~10 GiB/GPU (FP8,
# reduced util/ctx) leaving very little headroom for both KV pools — cut
# this profile's VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN and VLLM_GPU_MEM_UTIL back down for
# coexistence (see git history for the earlier 0.58 util / 8192 ctx
# coexist values).
#
# Requires the /srv/llm compose volume mount and patch-v2 ple-cpu-offload in
# the flashnext image (ec99be9+).
# Run: FLASHNEXT_PROFILE=qwen3.8-flash-next-aixiaoma just up-flashnext
#
# 2026-08-29: three holistic-review corrections (research pass comparing our
# config against recipes.vllm.ai/Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next and the model's own
# HF config.json), all env-only, no rebuild required. LIVE-VALIDATED on
# giant-server: clean boot, coherence check passed, 2x concurrent request
# probe passed (no #35288-style corruption), and re-benched vs the prior
# cudagraph-decode-only baseline (tg32 74.07±5.13, tg128 69.68±10.59):
#   tg32 73.28±2.79, tg128 69.60±2.52, pp2048 2383.66±1.93 — within noise,
#   no regression.
#
# 1. VLLM_SPEC_DECODE below overrides env/qwen3.6.env.common's
#    num_speculative_tokens=4 down to 3. That default was tuned for the
#    qwen3.6-27b MTP4 profile; flash-next's checkpoint has a SINGLE MTP head
#    (text_config.mtp.num_hidden_layers=1 / mtp_num_hidden_layers=1, per the
#    model's HF config.json), and the official recipe validates and
#    recommends num_speculative_tokens=3 ("TP4 and TEP4 are validated ...
#    including with MTP3"). Running depth 4 on a 1-layer MTP head loops one
#    step past what Qwen validated: strictly wasted draft+verify compute at
#    the lowest-acceptance tail, plus unnecessary extra exposure to the
#    #35288 4+-decode-sequence corruption bug this repo caps VLLM_MAX_NUM_SEQS
#    for. RESULT: tg32/tg128 unchanged within noise (no measurable win or
#    loss) — kept anyway since it matches the recipe-validated depth and
#    removes the extra, unvalidated MTP step.
#
# 2. PREFIX_CACHE_RETENTION_INTERVAL is new here (mirrors the workaround
#    already applied on qwen3.6-27b/qwen3.8-27b/qwen3.6-35b-a3b — see the
#    full note in env/qwen3.8-27b.env): pins dense Mamba/GDN prefix-cache
#    retention to the attention block size so the v0.28.1rc0+ sparse default
#    doesn't drop checkpoints under MTP (#53504). CONFIRMED value: this
#    profile's own scheduler_block_size is 800 (NOT 832 — that's the
#    qwen3.6/3.8-27b value; flash-next's GDN geometry differs). 832 was
#    tried first as a starting guess and vLLM raised a hard boot-time
#    ValueError ("prefix_cache_retention_interval (832) must be
#    non-negative and a multiple of scheduler_block_size (800)"), which is
#    how 800 was determined. RESULT: re-ran benchmarks/prefix_cache_probe.py
#    (4-turn growing-prefix probe) post-fix — still a flat 0%
#    prefix_cache_hits_total (queries up to 329, hits 0). The 1x-block-size
#    retention interval alone does NOT unblock #45238 on our stack/pin
#    (unlike the-crypt-keeper's #53899 NVIDIA report at 3x their block size
#    getting 85-100% hits — that report is on a different vLLM
#    build/hardware and the underlying align-mode checkpoint bug is still
#    open upstream on our pin). Kept anyway: it's a documented, harmless
#    correctness setting recommended for v0.28.1rc0+ MTP, and a future probe
#    at 1600/2400 (3x) or a pin bump that includes the real #45238 fix may
#    still move this — do not re-derive 800 by trial and error again, reuse
#    this value.
#
# 3. VLLM_MAX_NUM_SEQS raised 1->2 (still well under the universal #35288
#    cap of 2 that env/qwen3.6.env.common documents). This profile ran at 1
#    (zero batching) with no documented reason tied to flash-next
#    specifically; the recipe runs max-num-seqs 256 with MTP3 on TP4. RESULT:
#    2x concurrent chat-completion requests both completed coherently with
#    no crash/corruption; kept at 2 as a conservative first step above 1.
VLLM_MODEL=/srv/llm/aixiaoma/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-W4A16
VLLM_TOKENIZER=/srv/llm/aixiaoma/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-W4A16
VLLM_SERVED_NAME=qwen3.8-flash-next-aixiaoma
VLLM_TP=4
VLLM_GPU_MEM_UTIL=0.98
VLLM_KV_CACHE_DTYPE=bfloat16
# max_split_size_mb:512 caps the HIP caching-allocator block-split size so it
# releases the large over-reserved segments the profiling pass leaves cached
# (see the 2026-08-29 POSITIVE-RESULT note above): consumed 26.78 -> 20.45
# GiB/GPU, KV pool 202,581 -> 635,139 tok (conc 4.10x), coherent, graphs on,
# perf within noise. This is the SAFE alternative to expandable_segments
# (which corrupts output on this stack -- see NEGATIVE-RESULT note above).
PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=max_split_size_mb:512
# 2026-08-30: raised 155000 -> 204800 to hold a full coding-agent budget
# (client COPILOT_PROVIDER_MAX_PROMPT_TOKENS=180000 + MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=20000
# = 200000). When prompt+output exceeded the old 155000 cap, long sessions
# overflowed and got left-truncated, dropping the earliest turns -> the model
# re-derived the same step repeatedly (reasoning-loop symptom). Affordable
# after the max_split_size KV-pool win: 635,139-tok pool / 204800 = 3.10x
# concurrency, still >= max_num_seqs=2. Model supports 262K natively.
#
# 2026-08-30 (later): raised 204800 -> 262144 (the model's full native
# context) after confirming this is NOT a KV-memory-limited decision: on the
# rebased fb97542c pin, GPU KV cache size is 651,367 tokens, which alone
# gives 651,367 / 262144 = 2.48x concurrency -- still comfortably above
# VLLM_MAX_NUM_SEQS=2 even at the full native length. The 204800 figure was
# only ever a client-budget match (180k+20k), never a technical ceiling.
VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN=262144
VLLM_MAX_BATCHED_TOKENS=2048
VLLM_MAX_NUM_SEQS=2
VLLM_PLE_CPU_OFFLOAD=1
VLLM_SPEC_DECODE='{"method": "mtp", "num_speculative_tokens": 3, "attention_backend": "ROCM_AITER_UNIFIED_ATTN"}'
PREFIX_CACHE_RETENTION_INTERVAL=800
# Unquoted + space-free JSON: compose strips quotes only at value start and
# the entrypoint word-splits VLLM_EXTRA_ARGS.
VLLM_EXTRA_ARGS=--enable-expert-parallel --compilation-config {"mode":0,"cudagraph_mode":"FULL_DECODE_ONLY"}
